WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:03.683
- Hey, everyone.

00:00:03.683 --> 00:00:05.760
Welcome, welcome, welcome

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.270
into another edition of "Market Takes".

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.680
I know you could've been
anywhere in the world,

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.870
but you're here with me,
and I appreciate that.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.430
I'm your host, Dion Rabouin,

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.440
reporter at "The Wall Street Journal".

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.450
This is "Market Takes".

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.220
There's a lot going on out in the world,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.770
but we are going to tell
you the three things

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.470
you need to be watching for this week

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.230
as you look to make sense of
what's going on in the world.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.090
So, big thing, obviously,

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.250
you've been hearing all
about it in the news,

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.270
seeing all the headlines,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.130
that's this Debt Ceiling debate.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.440
We're going to breakdown
what's actually happening.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.140
Make a little sense of all this hoopla

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.060
that you're hearing out there in the news.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.380
We are going to talk
through some important

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.420
housing reports that are
coming out this week.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.550
We are seeing some very
significant movement

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:48.990
in the housing market that, I think,

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.520
bears watching out this week.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.150
And then we are going to
get just a deluge of U.S.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.760
Business Conditions report
from the Federal Reserve

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.363
and from others this week.
That I think is really going to

00:00:59.363 --> 00:01:00.780
tell us some interesting things

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.920
about the state of the economy.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.740
You're going to want to know
what to watch out for there.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.630
Listen, let's just
really quickly step back

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:07.710
and talk about the Debt Ceiling.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.210
Let's separate truth from fiction,

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.670
and a little bit of all the blase blah

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.310
that you've been hearing about
in the headlines right now.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.290
So the Debt Ceiling,

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.610
the important thing to keep in mind

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.290
is we don't have to argue about this.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.180
Congress doesn't have to argue about this.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.830
This is a chosen battle.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.970
The Debt Ceiling has been
raised 78 times since 1962;

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:28.920
49 of those under Republican presidents,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.900
29 of those under Democratic presidents.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.290
It usually happens without
a lot of back and forth.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.910
This year we've got a standoff,

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.890
and we've got this standstill.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.600
We are having negotiations now.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.760
We are approaching June 1st.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.730
That June 1st X-date
that Treasury Secretary,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.710
Janet Yellen has said when the treasury

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.660
will start to run out of funding

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.870
and have to not pay
some of its obligations.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.260
That's happened because we already,

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.580
essentially, hit the Debt Ceiling number.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.250
We reached the $31.4 trillion

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.800
debt limit back in late January,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.690
and the Treasury Department
has already been taking

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.150
extraordinary measures to keep the country

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.970
from not paying some of its obligations.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.860
Now important to talk about,

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.120
you're hearing a lot of talk

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.100
about defaulting and default.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.510
That doesn't mean the U.S. isn't going

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.910
to pay the interest on
the money that it owes.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.380
It doesn't mean there's actually

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.020
going to be a default on U.S. debt.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.610
Default, in the way that a lot

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.320
of folks have been using that term,

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.690
literally just means that some
things might not get paid.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.910
Some social security
beneficiaries might not get paid.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:37.080
Some Medicare beneficiaries,
VA veteran assistance,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.420
things like that, or Obamacare,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.810
things that the government
is on the hook to pay for,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:43.830
that they are mandated pay for,

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.870
they might not make a payment on.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.410
The treasury is going to
prioritize paying the debt,

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.270
because if the U.S. doesn't pay its debt,

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.460
well, that could mean a debt default,

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.740
which could mean a downgrade
of the U.S. credit limit,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.050
which could mean that it
costs significantly more

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.240
for the U.S. to borrow,
which would kind of raise

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.880
the rates for borrowing across
the board in the economy.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.170
That would be very bad.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.000
The treasury is going to
do everything in its power

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.060
to avoid that which means
likely not paying back

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.610
some of these other things
rather than defaulting

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.830
or having an actual default on the debt.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.590
So, really quickly let's
take a look at the timeline.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:17.970
April 18th was tax day.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.310
We all know that because
we had to get the taxes in.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.860
Secretary Yellen based her estimates

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.230
of what the treasury has to pay for things

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:28.440
on what the tax receipts
estimates were for that.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.360
Now CBO and treasury

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.130
are still kind of counting
some of these receipts,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.020
but we have an idea of how much money

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.440
came in from taxes since
that April 18th number.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.300
But no one outside of
the Treasury Department

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.560
actually knows how much money

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.900
because again they haven't told us.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.190
This is really just an estimate.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.880
Now the Congressional Budget
Office, the CBO has estimated,

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.650
and they've said that receipts
in April 2023 were 26% less

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.570
than the amount recorded in
the same month last year,

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.580
which means based on
the estimates they have,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.300
they actually got less in
taxes than they expected

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.500
and less than last year.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.220
Now we've seen that was driven

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.340
by a 36% decline in
individual income taxes.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.257
CBO's projections show
a federal budget deficit

00:04:11.257 --> 00:04:14.070
$100 billion more than estimated.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.500
Basically, they got less from taxes,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.060
largely from individuals,

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.447
and now they're saying,

00:04:19.447 --> 00:04:21.870
"Okay, well, we've got
less money to deal with,"

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.550
so we could be actually closer

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.710
to that sort of default scenario

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.810
or not paying back certain,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.390
or not paying for certain obligations

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.190
than that June 1st deadline even.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.260
So, again, when we hit June,

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.060
it's likely if we don't
get a deal on this,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.410
some things are not going to be paid.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.240
You're not going to see
a government shutdown

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.950
like we saw back in 2011,

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.510
but you could see some folks

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.070
not getting money that's expected.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.650
Some agencies not getting
money that's expected.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.390
Congress is scheduled to be out

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.460
for a state work period starting May 29th.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.030
So it may be if there's
no deal today or tomorrow,

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.670
that because of the way the
Senate and the House work,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.620
that they may not even be able to get,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.150
even if they agree to something,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.300
something passed before
that June 1st deadline.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.973
And that could mean that
some things don't get paid.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.840
Congress is supposed to be
out from May 29th to June 5th.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.520
So that whole week that's both the House

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.870
and the Senate are supposed to be out.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:13.800
They're scheduled to be out.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.830
Obviously, they could just not
take their state work period,

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.110
and stay in Congress and
get a deal hammered out.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:20.430
June 15th is important,

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.860
because that's when you get
quarterly tax payments due.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:24.687
A lot of that money from corporations,

00:05:24.687 --> 00:05:27.270
and some money from individual
tax payers, businesses,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.060
business owners things
like that pay quarterly.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.410
So they'll get some more money.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.950
Expected to get a good
a good chunk of change.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.990
So the treasury has to sort
of just make things happen

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.930
between June 1st and June 15th

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.790
when they'll get some
more money rolling in.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.800
On June 30th the treasury
is allowed too extract

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.170
about $140 billion in borrowing power

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.720
from a key federal retirement fund.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.000
So there's still money coming in.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.350
There's still going to be money coming in,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.850
but there's a lot of money going out,

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.100
and no one quite knows how much it is.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.300
But we do know that we've got

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.130
less than expected from taxes

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.950
and less than expected from some

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:03.990
of those payments that
were expected to come in.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.690
Really we are only likely
to reach a default,

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:09.090
as in the U.S. doesn't
pay its debt obligations,

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.360
in August or September
because of all those

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.940
influxes and outflows of money.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.640
Before we get to August or September,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.410
there is money for things to be paid,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.000
but there is not enough money.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.360
And no one knows exactly
how much money there is.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.100
Once we hit October,

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.560
an actual default of
multiple debt obligations

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:29.580
become very likely.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.520
But again, we are in May right now.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.510
You've still got June.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:34.343
You've still got July.

00:06:34.343 --> 00:06:35.250
You've still got August.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:36.090
You've still got September

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.410
before it is likely the U.S.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.380
just absolutely defaults on its bonds.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.230
However, the market is already responding,

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.800
and we're not seeing much of a
response in the stock market.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.570
And I'm going to show you
right now really quickly.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:50.130
I want to put this up on your screen

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:52.650
what we're seeing in the market right now.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.930
What you're seeing up there
on your screen is the NASDAQ,

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.030
and we have really seen the NASDAQ pickup.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.110
This chart you're seeing here

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:00.450
is the year-to-date
response of the NASDAQ,

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:02.040
and you can see we're
really starting to pick up.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.050
Stocks have really started to increase,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.190
and you're seeing prices rise,

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.080
which shows you investors
on the stock side

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.100
really not that worried.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.870
But where we are seeing some worry

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.970
is in the U.S. Treasury market,

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.530
and not on these long-dated bonds.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.300
You're seeing here there is some worries

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.940
about the Debt Ceiling
deal not coming through,

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.340
but if you look at the
30-year, pretty muted.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:21.930
You're seeing a one basis point move

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:24.480
on the 10-year note, the
most actively traded,

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:26.700
but look down here on this one month note.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:27.960
Boom, look at that.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.610
Just a huge pickup.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.710
And that's because if there's no deal,

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.790
the idea is that that June
treasury bond might not be paid

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.010
or that June treasury
bill may not get paid.

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:41.040
And you are seeing folks sell
that off up 15 basis points.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.010
We know when yields rise, prices fall.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:45.330
That means people are selling.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:46.163
They don't want it.

00:07:46.163 --> 00:07:49.050
They're trying to get out of
that one month treasury bill,

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.540
and you are seeing a
significant market reaction.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.520
I want to show you that year-to-date,

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.650
and you really just see it pick up here,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.290
and you've seen that since
about late April, early May.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:01.530
Just a significant increase in the value

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.620
or the yield on that
one month treasury bill.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.440
So that just illustrating
how the market is responding

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.760
to this Debt Ceiling debate,

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.640
and we are seeing a
significant market response.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.610
So let's take a step back
from this Debt Ceiling thing,

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:15.613
because I know you probably
heard all about that.

00:08:15.613 --> 00:08:18.060
I just wanted to inject a
little reality in there.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.310
The housing market
reports that we have seen

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.740
last month's existing
home sales report showed

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.200
the largest annual price
decline in 11 years.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.300
However, that was mostly in the expensive

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.490
over $500,000 houses.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.410
Houses below $500,000,

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.290
which is most houses on the
existing home sales market,

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:37.500
still seeing a lot of demand,
still seeing prices rise.

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.810
Want to throw another chart
up here on your screen,

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:42.240
and this is the New Home Sales report.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.070
And now what we're seeing there

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:47.820
is an increase in the
number of new homes sold.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:50.760
Last month we saw the
March report come in,

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.540
and that was the highest
number of new homes sold

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:58.230
in a month in about a year
going back to April of 2022.

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:00.870
Now it looks like that
September number is close,

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:01.703
but it's not.

00:09:01.703 --> 00:09:04.980
You've seen actually the
number of homes sold,

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:06.810
of new homes sold in...

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.850
I'm sorry, the number of
new homes sold in April

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:11.490
was the highest since April of last year.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:14.340
So really a big increase
in new homes sold.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.930
And that chart illustrates there

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:19.020
the pickup we have seen in new homes sold.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:19.920
Will that continue?

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.380
That's a big question that the
Fed is going to want to know.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.820
That markets are going to want to know.

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:25.530
And we are seeing an increase in supply,

